From b37bce5074065b202629967d7689c4330cb9c4ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Danylo Storozhev <office@storozhev.net>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:25:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/18] [Bugfix][Spec Decode][Structured Output] Fail-closed
 drafts past the constrained bitmask rows (port PR #54442)

Port of upstream vLLM PR #54442 (ec1125926) to our base. Fixes the recurring
"grammar rejected tokens" / "Failed to advance FSM ... Terminating request"
failures on grammar-constrained requests (structural-tag tool calls) running
under DSpark speculative decoding.

Root cause: when a draft hand-off drops tokens, the grammar bitmask fills every
row after the first -1 placeholder with the all-permissive _full_mask, so the
rejection sampler can accept a draft that was verified against an unconstrained
row. The grammar later rejects those accepted tokens and the request is killed.

Fix (fail-closed at the rejection sampler, independent of the scheduler in use):
the scheduler records num_acceptable_drafts per request (leading drafts the
bitmask actually constrained, via strip_speculative_padding); the model runner
computes the draft positions past that index; the rejection sampler verifies
against a copy of draft_sampled with those positions set to -1, pinning the
accepted length before it reaches a permissive row. Real draft ids are untouched
for sampling params and watermarking.

Unlike our 0015 (which defers the bitmask in AsyncScheduler), this runs in the
plain Scheduler path this server actually uses (async scheduling is off), so it
covers the failing case. num_acceptable_drafts defaults to None (older
scheduler / warmup), handled conservatively by invalidating the whole window.

Hand-merge notes: output.py / batch_shard.py / rejection_sampler.py applied from
the PR unchanged; scheduler.py and model_runner.py hand-merged around our sm_80
patches; strip_speculative_padding added to structured_output/utils.py (absent
from our base). Helpers unit-tested; imports and GrammarOutput back-compat
verified. Python-only, bind-mounted like 0009.
